\end_inset

Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly answer research questions by calling tools,
 yet their answers can still contain claims that no tool output supports,
 and a reader cannot easily tell which claims are grounded.
 I present epistaint,
 an epistemic taint-tracking harness that records every tool call an agent makes outside the model's control and audits its final answer claim by claim.
 A transparent Model Context Protocol (MCP) proxy logs each tool result with a trace id,
 a trust tier set by a policy file and a hash-chained digest,
 and tags the result so that the agent can cite it.
 An auditor then checks every claim:
 cited trace ids must exist,
 quotations and numbers must appear in the cited output,
 uncited claims are labelled synthetic,
 and each claim inherits the least trustworthy tier among its sources.
 On six questions about a fictional research corpus answered by Claude Opus 5.5,
 the agent's claims were well supported in every condition,
 but provenance linked 74% of its claims to specific evidence,
 and 92% after one round of audit feedback,
 whereas without provenance only about two-thirds of claims could be checked at all.
 In an error-injection test on 229 real claims,
 the audit caught every fabricated citation,
 94% of changed numbers and 90% of misattributed citations with checkable content,
 with no false positives.
 The harness does not prevent hallucinations;
 it makes them visible.
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

