Large Language Models (LLMs) are often used for general queries as well as research,
 gaining popularity due to the ability to effectively search for information using natural language.
 Such advancements can make research quicker and easier than ever,
 however it is important that the research generated is factually accurate.
 Whilst most modern LLM models perform quite well when it comes to being factually accurate,
 to this day it is not unheard of for them to hallucinate false information,
 or even,
 much like humans,
 misinterpret information and thus provide an answer that,
 whilst being a correct answer in its original context,
 is not a correct answer to the question they were asked.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Whilst ensuring full epistemic truth is generally not a realistic goal,
 tracking every claim an LLM makes,
 ensuring that it both has an attached citation & correctly references that citation,
 should help prevent hallucination from being a factor in misinformation.
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

