In order to track the claims made by LLMs,
 I created a python harness that traces every claim and ensures that they all can be traced to accurately referenced citations.
 The goal is not to prevent fabricated claims from being made,
 but rather to flag them,
 giving a hypothetical user the freedom to then decide what to do with the knowledge that information they were provided is not epistemically sound.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The produced harness allows you to trace any claim an LLM makes via trace IDs.
 The data itself carries no trace IDs:
 the proxy assigns a fresh one to every tool result at the moment the tool is called and logs it,
 so the harness works with any MCP tool server and any data,
 without changes to either.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Verifying the first sweep by hand turned up an auditor bug.
 It read CSV (Comma Separated Values) commas as thousands separators,
 which made real values look unanchored.
 This was fixed and re-scored based on the saved answers without any new model calls.
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

