To test the harness,
 I gave an agent six questions about a small,
 fictional research corpus:
 the field-trial notes and results table of an invented bird-call classifier,
 “Project Kestrel”,
 with three model versions (v1 to v3) evaluated at four sites.
 Because the corpus is fictional,
 the agent cannot answer from its training data,
 so any claim that no tool output supports is unsupported by construction.
 The corpus also contains traps:
 an unverified forum post claiming that v3 gets “97% accuracy everywhere”,
 validation results next to the test results that the deployment criterion actually uses,
 a site score that the trial lead says should not be trusted,
 and a question that the corpus cannot answer.
 The six questions were:
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Does any model version meet the deployment criterion from the design review?
 Give the numbers that decide it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What accuracy does the v3 model achieve?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How many recordings were lost at Ashby Fen,
 when,
 and why?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compare the false positive rates of v2 and v3 at Ashby Moor on the test split,
 and explain the difference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What did the design review decide about each model version,
 and who said “No model ships on validation numbers alone”?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is the v3 classifier head worth its extra inference cost on the field hardware?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The agent was Claude Opus 5.5 running in Claude Code in headless mode,
 with its built-in tools disabled,
 so that the only way it could gather evidence was through the corpus tools behind the proxy.
 This model was chosen as it was readily available,
 however with more time and resources a larger range of models could be tested.
 Each question was asked once under each of three conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Baseline:

\series default
 the proxy logged every tool call but did not tag the results,
 and the prompt did not ask for citations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Provenance:

\series default
 every result was tagged with its trace id,
 and the prompt asked for every factual sentence to cite the trace ids it relies on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Enforce:

\series default
 as provenance,
 but the audit's feedback was sent back to the agent once,
 and its revised answer was scored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 18 runs made 140 tool calls and cost about $2 in total,
 therefore it should be feasible for most individuals to reproduce these results.
 Every final answer was then checked in two ways,
