\begin_layout Standard
Asking the agent to cite its evidence did not make it noticeably more accurate:
 in all three conditions,
 95–100% of the claims that contain a number or a quotation can be found in what the agent actually saw,
 and at this sample size the differences between conditions are not meaningful.
 What provenance changes is how much of an answer can be checked,
 and how precisely.
 Without it,
 only the claims that contain a number or a quotation (about two-thirds) can be checked at all,
 and only against the session as a whole,
 so a figure is accepted if it appears anywhere the agent looked.
 With provenance,
 74% of the claims,
 and 92% after one round of feedback,
 are linked to the specific trace they rely on,
 and those that contain a number or quotation are checked against that trace:
 60 of 62 and 64 of 65 matched.
 A cited claim with no number or quotation,
 such as “The review keeps v2 as the production candidate until that re-score”,
 is linked to its source but cannot be verified mechanically.
 The claims that remained uncited were mostly the agent's own conclusions,
 such as “None of the three model versions meets the deployment criterion on the current data”:
 they are reasoned from the evidence rather than read from it,
 and the audit labels them T3 instead of letting them pass as verified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I checked every flagged claim by hand.
 All three cited claims that did not match their trace were under-citations:
 the agent compared a value with one of the deployment thresholds (0.060 or 0.870),
 which come from the design review,
 but cited only the SQL output,
 so the threshold was not in the cited evidence.
 In the baseline,
 two of the three claims whose content appears in no tool output were differences that the agent had worked out itself (correctly,
 but with no tool output to verify them),
 and the third put a paraphrase in quotation marks (“97% everywhere” for the forum's “97% accuracy everywhere”).
 The one such claim under enforce was the agent commenting on its own revision (“I fixed claim 7 ...”),
 not a claim about the corpus.
 In this small sample the model did not invent facts;
 hallucinations remain possible,
 and what the harness adds is that each claim is either tied to the evidence it relies on or flagged.
 All twelve claims that relied on the unverified forum post (tier T2) reported or rejected it rather than relying on it as true,
 but the label still shows a reader exactly which sentences rest on an unverified source.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the agent made few mistakes on its own,
 I also measured whether mistakes are caught when they do happen.
 I took every claim that the audit had marked as grounded with its own citation (229 claims from 25 answers,
 including a pilot run and the first drafts of the enforce condition) and made copies with one planted error each:
 a citation replaced with a trace id that does not exist,
 one digit of one number changed,
 one word of one quotation changed,
 the citations removed,
 or the citation swapped for a different,
 real trace from the same session.
 
