\begin_layout Standard
Neither check flagged an unmodified claim,
 and both caught every changed quotation.
 The per-claim audit caught 94% of changed numbers against 83% for the session-wide check:
 the session-wide check only catches a changed value if it appears nowhere in the session,
 while the per-claim audit requires it to appear in the trace the claim cites.
 The seven changed numbers that the per-claim audit missed had all been altered to a value that also appears elsewhere in the cited table,
 because numbers are matched against the cited output as a set of values rather than tied to a particular row.
 Errors in the citations themselves can only be detected with provenance:
 the audit caught every fabricated and removed citation,
 and 90% of wrong citations when the claim contains a number or a quotation,
 but a claim with nothing checkable in it (for example “Validation numbers don't count”) can only be checked for whether its citation exists.
 Catching those,
 and catching the misinterpretations described in the introduction,
 would need a semantic check on top of the provenance log.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each question was asked once per condition,
 with a single model and a small fictional corpus,
 so these results show that the approach works as intended rather than measure how often it is needed in practice,
 and I do not claim statistical significance.
\end_layout

