\begin_layout Section*
Limitations and Dual-Use / Ethical Considerations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What this work does and does not establish.
 The harness records an agent's tool calls in a tamper-evident log and flags claims that are uncited,
 cite traces that do not exist,
 or contain numbers or quotations missing from the evidence they cite;
 on a small fictional corpus it detected planted errors of these kinds reliably,
 with no false positives.
 It does not check whether a claim interprets its evidence correctly:
 a number taken from the right source but used in the wrong context (for example,
 validation rather than test accuracy) passes.
 Claims without numbers or quotations can only be checked for whether their citation exists,
 and numbers are matched as a set of values,
 so a changed number that appears elsewhere in the cited output passes.
 A trust tier describes how evidence was obtained,
 not whether the source is right.
 The results come from one model,
 six questions,
 one run per condition and a fictional corpus,
 so they show that the approach works rather than how often it is needed,
 and no statistical significance is claimed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dual use.
 A “grounded” label could lend unwarranted authority to a claim:
 an agent can cite a real trace that does not support what it says,
 and a reader might treat the label as proof.
 Tiers come from a policy file,
 so whoever writes the policy decides what counts as trustworthy,
 and a misleading policy could make untrusted sources look deterministic.
 The log of tool calls could also be used to monitor what users and agents read.
 To limit this,
 the audit reports what was and was not checked for each claim rather than a single verdict,
 and the policy is a plain,
 inspectable file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Personal data.
 No personal data was used.
 The corpus is fictional,
 and the only external service involved was the Claude model,
 accessed through Claude Code.
\end_layout

